# Branch hygiene: master vs git-ai/automation-route

## Non-negotiable rule

- `master` must remain **product-only** (frontend/backend source + minimal docs needed for development).
- All AI automation iteration artifacts must live **only** on the `git-ai/automation-route` branch.

## What counts as “automation artifacts”

These are allowed on `git-ai/automation-route` but must **not** be added/modified on `master`:

- `git-ai/**` (plans, runs, backlog, business status, iteration logs)
- `scripts/smoke-*.ps1`, `scripts/*oneclick*.ps1`, `scripts/*heartbeat*.ps1`
- Any generated run logs / snapshots (e.g. `**/runs/**`, `**/history/**`, `*.full.md`, `*-rerun.md`)
- Any temporary test data dumps for automation (e.g. `*.json` produced by runs)

If there is doubt whether a file is “automation artifact”, default to **keeping it off `master`**.

## When working on master

- Do **not** create/edit files under the “automation artifacts” patterns above.
- If the task requires automation changes, switch to the `git-ai/automation-route` branch first, then do the work there.
- If you accidentally touched automation artifacts while on `master`, revert those changes and keep only product changes.

## When working on git-ai/automation-route

- You may add/update `git-ai/**` and automation scripts freely.
- Prefer to keep automation changes isolated from product code; if product code must change, keep commits clearly separated and explain why.

## PR / merge policy (enforceable expectation)

- Never merge `git-ai/automation-route` into `master` wholesale.
- If `master` needs a product fix discovered via automation, cherry-pick only the product commits (no `git-ai/**` and no automation logs).

